  "id": "dumbbell-plank-row",
  "name": "Dumbbell Plank Row",
  "definition": "An alternating dumbbell row from a high plank with two stable dumbbells beneath the shoulders and both feet about shoulder-width apart. One arm pulls toward the side of the torso while the opposite grounded dumbbell and both forefeet support the body; the working dumbbell returns to the floor before the other arm rows. Shoulder extension from flexion, scapular retraction, and elbow flexion produce the row. The spinal plane basis describes resisted extension and rotation, not prescribed trunk movement. UConn Health explicitly separates a push-up as an advanced modification; this family admits only the rowing repetition. The upper-arm-at-torso endpoint, neutral grip, stable non-rolling equal dumbbells, single-dumbbell load, and individual-row counting are disclosed catalog narrowings or logging conventions. Muscle roles combine the technical fixture with existing anatomy and supporting plank evidence; they are not exact-fixture EMG rankings or measured force shares. Shoulder taps, knee touches, floor drags, bench-supported rows, deliberate rotational rows, knee-supported rows, kettlebell versions, static planks, and row-plus-push-up combinations remain outside this contract.",
